{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Wrath, etc.</b> Some say these too are words of HaShem, and the meaning is: I have no great wrath." "<b>Would that I had [briers and thorns].</b> The verse speaks in the manner of men. The bet is elided, as in כי ששת ימים (Exodus 21:11). Likewise here: Would that I had briers and thorns that are in the vineyard. The meaning concerns the wicked. And 'on the day of my battle in the vineyard,' I would breach it, as in כי כפשע (1 Samuel 20:3)." "<b>I would set fire.</b> I would ignite, as in למה הציתו עבדיך (2 Samuel 14:31)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra reads אפשעה בה ('I would breach it') as continuing the wish; the syntax is compressed and the referent of the feminine suffix is ambiguous."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:55:25.429577732Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}